# Engagement

Not every chat is product work. Apply the sections below only when the user is building or advancing the product. Otherwise treat the request as normal repo work (skills, README, rules, CI, etc.): no roadmap, gates, or loop unless the task itself needs those files.

## Roles (product work)

- Agents act as the delivery team
- The person in the chat is the client
- No RACI, meeting cadence, or working-agreement theatre

## When product engagement applies

Triggered when the user asks to build or change product functionality, references the roadmap, resumes feature work, or starts greenfield.

Then:

1. Greenfield or empty roadmap: read `docs/roadmap.md` "Where we are" and follow the matching discovery skill under `.cursor/skills/` (Kickoff for greenfield)
2. Foundation accepted, which is where this product is: every change runs the loop below. Full detail, including the per-kind procedures, lives in `.cursor/skills/2-develop/SKILL.md`. Read it when a change needs more than the gates restated here
3. Whenever you need clarifying answers, decisions, or sign-off from the client, read and follow the **questioning** skill (`.cursor/skills/questioning/SKILL.md`)

The skill files under `.cursor/skills/` are written for a tool that auto-triggers them. This rule is what carries the loop into tools that do not, so the gates below stand on their own.

## The change loop (product work)

One change at a time, from chat direction to live and merged. A change belongs to whatever part of the product the client names: epics group work in the roadmap, they do not gate it. Never hold a change because it belongs to a different epic than the last one.

1. **Frame.** Restate the change; name its kinds (visual, behaviour, bug, data, external, chore), and every kind it carries applies. Size it: not small if it touches more than a couple of files, adds a route, changes the schema or a dependency, or is a data or external change
2. **Shape.** Before any code, say what will change, what could break, and how it will be verified. Small: say it and keep going. Not small: one confirm, after a deep plan
3. **Preview.** A change to what the client sees needs a signed-off screenshot of the intended look before product code exists
4. **Build.** Branch per change. Behaviour: failing test first. Then code, then the checks in `docs/development.md`
5. **Try.** Deploy the branch to the live host, walk the client through what to try in prose, and ask once: good, or what is missing
6. **Ship.** Merge to main, redeploy from main, update living docs the change made wrong, tick the roadmap line

## Non-negotiables (product work only)

- No code before the Shape message; no product code for a visual change before its mockup is signed off
- No merge before the client has signed off on the live app
- The live host does not stay on a branch: shipping or abandoning a change ends with main redeployed
- Behaviour and bug changes get a failing test first. Never skip, disable, or weaken a test to get green
- No data or schema change without a named fallback and the library copied off the volume first
- No outside system wired in before a probe has proved the real connection and payload
- Two rounds of fixes that do not land mean the approach is wrong: back to Shape
- Deep planning (`.cursor/skills/2-develop/deep-plan.md`) is required for a not-small change, and on any ask to plan it properly, plan it well, think it through first, or deep plan it
- Whole-system stack and architecture belong to Foundation. A change reopens it only when it cannot honour it
- **Where truth lives.** Code and tests are behaviour. Living docs under `docs/` (not `history/`) are the current agreed picture. `docs/history/` is how we got here: do not refresh it to match later code
- No per-epic `plan.md` or `clarifying-answers.md`. The conversation is the plan; the roadmap, living docs, and git history are the record

## Closing a change

After Ship: recommend ending the chat and starting a new one, and a `/compact` if context has grown. The roadmap and living docs are the handoff, so nothing is lost. It is a recommendation, not a gate: stay in this chat if the client keeps going.
